{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "moves.spike.tirewear_mount",
    "description": "PLAN.md step 7c, the CONFIGURATION half, and since 2026-09-15 the middle of three documents. This one holds no logic and no shape: the 57 `data_sources` `onroad/model.esm` reads under this fixture -- `fixtures/process-tirewear.esm`'s own, under the catalogue's names -- and one top-level `models` entry that is a `{ref}` to `onroad/model.esm`. It is mounted in turn by `tirewear_fixture.esm`, the per-fixture document. Every line of it is the inlined fixture's own, except the 57 `url_template`s, which gained one `../` because this file sits one directory deeper than `fixtures/`.\n\nWHAT IT MEASURES. PLAN.md §7.1 calls the per-fixture document `thin`, and since EarthSciAST PR #317 it is: `tirewear_fixture.esm` mounts THIS document, holds nothing but metadata, the enum union and one `{ref}`, and emits the same 375 rows byte for byte. This file stands in for the shared domain document's data half — with §7.3's monolithic database its catalogue is identical for every fixture, which is what lets it be shared rather than copied. See docs/esm-conventions.md §53.2.\n\nTHE 250-LINE `index_sets` BLOCK IS GONE, AND THAT IS WHAT CHANGED ON 2026-09-12. This document used to restate the logic half's 53 index sets verbatim, because the ingesting build read `file.index_sets` — this document's own registry — and a mounted child's did not enter it (finding F46). EarthSciAST PR #298 closed that: a mounted leaf is resolved in its own scope, its axes reach the build, and the leaf declares the metaparameters that size them. The stage header in run-tests.sh predicted this transition and said what to do at it; this is the document after doing it. F46, F48 and F49 retired together.\n\nTHE METAPARAMETERS WENT ON 2026-09-15. Until then all 49 were declared here AND in `onroad/model.esm`: this document's `data_sources` bind those names in `extent`, and the loader API closed discovered extents only at the ROOT, so deleting this copy passed `esm validate` and failed the build with `[template_import_unknown_name] loader API binds metaparameter(s) n_agecategory, ... but the document declares none`, and `bindings` could not carry them either (finding F50). EarthSciAST PR #317 binds a discovered extent in whichever document of the assembly declares the name, at either mount form and transitively. The 247-line block was deleted, 1255 lines became 1008, and the emitted relation did not move. F50 is retired.\n\nTHE ENUMS WENT ON 2026-09-17, and they were never needed for resolution. The logic half's `enums` reach the ingesting build on their own — change a value there and the emitted relation changes. This document restated the union only because `tools/check-conventions.py` required it: enums merged first-wins across a mount and a same-name symbol at a different value was applied SILENTLY, so an assembly that did not carry the union would have relabelled data with no error the day a second leaf was mounted (finding F13). EarthSciAST PR #353 resolves a mounted file's `enum` ops against that file's own block, the rule became `assembly-ref`, and the 31 lines are deleted here and in `tirewear_fixture.esm`.\n\n`index_set_rename` NOW WORKS AT THIS MOUNT EDGE and is checked here, though this document does not use it. It was refused until #298 (finding F49); re-measured since, renaming all 53 of the leaf's axes emits the same 375 rows byte for byte. It is what would let a per-fixture document namespace a shared domain document's axes rather than adopt its names, so run-tests.sh evaluates the rename rather than merely validating it.\n\nHOW IT IS CHECKED. By emitting its relation and diffing it against the inlined fixture's, which is the only verification this shape leaves (F45, F47): an asserting model beside the mount makes every assertion in the document error, and the mounted child's own tests do not run. 375 rows over 26 emitted fields, byte for byte, against the fixture stage's own output in the same run — so there is no recorded CSV to drift. `tirewear_fixture.esm` is checked the same way, one mount edge further out.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "moves",
      "onroad",
      "spike",
      "phase7",
      "run",
      "tirewear"
    ]
  },
